WEBVTT

00:00:00.288 --> 00:00:01.394
Today we’re looking at how people

00:00:01.394 --> 00:00:03.192
say I and you in Vietnamese.

00:00:03.298 --> 00:00:04.788
Imagine meeting an older friend for

00:00:04.788 --> 00:00:05.385
coffee.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:06.663
The words you use can depend

00:00:06.663 --> 00:00:07.654
on your relationship.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.404
Let’s follow one conversation and see

00:00:09.404 --> 00:00:10.192
how that works.

00:00:11.538 --> 00:00:12.654
In English, you can say you

00:00:12.654 --> 00:00:14.115
to an older friend, a younger

00:00:14.115 --> 00:00:15.596
colleague, or a stranger.

00:00:15.731 --> 00:00:17.615
Vietnamese often makes the relationship more

00:00:17.615 --> 00:00:18.423
explicit.

00:00:18.538 --> 00:00:20.288
Words used for family relationships can

00:00:20.288 --> 00:00:22.096
also address people outside the family.

00:00:23.186 --> 00:00:25.032
Take a simple adult conversation.

00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:26.609
When addressing an older man, a

00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:27.965
younger speaker might use the term

00:00:27.965 --> 00:00:29.647
shown here, which also means older

00:00:29.647 --> 00:00:30.263
brother.

00:00:30.388 --> 00:00:31.667
The speaker can refer to themself

00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:32.590
with the term for a younger

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:33.090
sibling.

00:00:34.244 --> 00:00:35.686
Now the older man speaks.

00:00:35.763 --> 00:00:36.917
He can use the older-brother term

00:00:36.917 --> 00:00:38.551
for himself and the younger-sibling term

00:00:38.551 --> 00:00:39.609
for the other person.

00:00:39.705 --> 00:00:41.013
The people have not changed.

00:00:41.119 --> 00:00:42.503
Who is speaking has changed, so

00:00:42.503 --> 00:00:44.311
the English translations switch between I

00:00:44.311 --> 00:00:44.763
and you.

00:00:45.853 --> 00:00:47.256
For an older woman, the older-sister

00:00:47.256 --> 00:00:48.795
term is another common choice.

00:00:48.872 --> 00:00:50.112
But this is not a universal

00:00:50.112 --> 00:00:51.122
age calculator.

00:00:51.247 --> 00:00:53.718
Familiarity, social roles, setting, and personal

00:00:53.718 --> 00:00:54.910
preference also matter.

00:00:55.035 --> 00:00:56.506
Other forms of address exist.

00:00:57.622 --> 00:00:59.449
Instead of memorizing a single translation

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:00.853
for you, learn how two people

00:01:00.853 --> 00:01:02.237
address each other in a particular

00:01:02.237 --> 00:01:03.064
situation.

00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:05.064
Notice both sides of the conversation.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:06.304
The words tell you something about

00:01:06.304 --> 00:01:07.574
the relationship as well as who

00:01:07.574 --> 00:01:08.276
is speaking.
